enum AboutText {
    static let json = #"""
{"future-plans":{"sections":[{"ground":"deep","blocks":[{"t":"eyebrow","text":"About","slot":"future-hero.eyebrow"},{"t":"title","text":"Future Plans","slot":"future-hero.title"},{"t":"lede","text":"A church, a youth centre with housing above it, and a diocese centre — three buildings on the land at 169 Rice Road, drawn and filed with the town. None of it is built. This is what has been designed, and how far it has got.","slot":"future-hero.lede"}]},{"ground":"plain","blocks":[{"t":"title","text":"Three buildings, one courtyard","slot":"what-is-being-built.h2"},{"t":"prose","paragraphs":["The church now worships in a building it did not build. It was put up for an Episcopal congregation, it was bought in 2016, and the altar, the screen and the icons in it were fitted into a room that was designed around a different service. [How that happened](/about/who-we-are) is its own story. What was always going to follow from it is this one.","The design puts three buildings around a paved court. The church stands at the east end, on the highest part of the site, with its doors facing the car park across a broad flight of steps. Behind it and to the north runs the long building that holds the Sunday school on its lower floors and one-bedroom apartments above. Between them and Rice Road sits the diocese centre, the smallest of the three.","They are one project and they are not one building. The church is drawn in the Coptic language of arches, vaults and a dome; the other two are plainly and deliberately not, so that a reader driving past can tell at a glance which of the three is the church.","It has been drawn by an architecture practice with eight consulting firms working under it — structural, civil, traffic, code, mechanical, landscape, wetland and survey — and the drawings have been in front of the Wayland Planning Board since August 2025."],"slot":"what-is-being-built.body"},{"t":"figures","items":[{"value":"119,458","label":"square feet","note":"Across all three buildings and every floor. TOTAL BUILDING AREA schedule, sheet R0."},{"value":"570","label":"seats","note":"The figure the parking requirement is calculated from on the site layout plan."},{"value":"190","label":"parking spaces","note":"One per three seats, which is what the Wayland bylaw asks of a church. Six accessible."},{"value":"5.5","label":"acres","note":"The existing lot. Map 40, Lot 008 — no land is being bought."}]}]},{"ground":"sunken","blocks":[{"t":"eyebrow","text":"Building A"},{"t":"title","text":"The church","slot":"the-church.h2"},{"t":"prose","paragraphs":["A nave of 5,429 square feet under barrel vaults, with a dome over the crossing and a gallery above the west end. Seating for 570 — about three times what the present building holds. The main altar stands at the east with a side altar to either side of it, which is the Coptic arrangement: three sanctuaries behind one screen, so that more than one Liturgy can be served on a morning when more than one is needed.","Around them the rooms a Liturgy actually needs and the present building improvises: a baptistery off the nave, a chamber for the relics, a room for the deacons to vest in, a chorus, and two crying rooms — one on each level — so that a parent with a small child can still see and hear the altar.","The floor of the church sits sixteen feet above the car park. That is the reason the approach is a flight of steps and the reason there is a ramp beside it, drawn at one in twelve with seven landings, so that the same doors are the way in for everybody.","It also means there is a whole floor underneath the nave, and that floor is where most of a Sunday actually happens: a dining hall of 4,050 square feet, a kitchen with a pantry and a receiving door behind it, a bakery for the bread, the bookstore, the three church offices, and a small chapel of 887 square feet for the weekday services that do not need 570 seats."],"slot":"the-church.body"}]},{"ground":"plain","blocks":[{"t":"eyebrow","text":"Building B"},{"t":"title","text":"Sunday school, sport, and housing","slot":"youth-and-housing.h2"},{"t":"prose","paragraphs":["The largest of the three at 60,458 square feet, and the one that carries most of what the church does between Sundays. Thirteen classrooms on the ground floor, from 487 to 665 square feet, with a nurse station and a staff kitchen among them and more Sunday school space on the floor below. A flexible room of 2,810 square feet, and a game room beside it.","Behind those, a full-size sports court of 7,400 square feet with locker rooms off it — a real court, marked for basketball, not a hall with a hoop at one end. It is the single largest room in the project after the nave.","Above all of it are twenty-one one-bedroom apartments across two floors, with a common room on each, reached by their own stairs and lift. They are drawn for older members of the congregation: housing on the same ground as the church, so that growing old does not mean leaving it."],"slot":"youth-and-housing.body"}]},{"ground":"sunken","blocks":[{"t":"eyebrow","text":"Building C"},{"t":"title","text":"The diocese centre","slot":"the-diocese-centre.h2"},{"t":"prose","paragraphs":["The smallest of the three, at 23,983 square feet over three levels, and the one that serves beyond this congregation. Its ground floor is the function room — 5,639 square feet drawn with twenty-four round tables in it, its own kitchen and its own lobby off the car park, so a wedding or a funeral reception does not have to be held in the room the Liturgy was just served in.","Above that, offices, a conference room, a lounge and three apartments. Below it, a hall with dressing rooms either side.","It stands closest to Rice Road, on roughly the ground the present church occupies — which is the plain reason the present church has to come down before this one can go up."],"slot":"the-diocese-centre.body"}]},{"ground":"tinted","blocks":[{"t":"eyebrow","text":"119,458 square feet, accounted for","slot":"all-of-it.eyebrow"},{"t":"title","text":"What the space is for","slot":"all-of-it.h2"},{"t":"lede","text":"Straight off the schedule that repeats on every floor plan in the filed set, summed across the three buildings. The seven lines add to the total exactly.","slot":"all-of-it.lede"},{"t":"list","items":[{"term":"Function hall and congregational needs","detail":"The dining hall, the function room, the kitchens, the lobbies and the servery. Basement and ground floor.","note":"27,736 square feet"},{"term":"Sunday school","detail":"Thirteen classrooms, a nurse station, lobbies and storage, on the basement and ground levels of Building B.","note":"24,055 square feet"},{"term":"Church and congregation","detail":"The nave, the altars, the baptistery, the chorus, the deacons’ room, the foyer and the two crying rooms.","note":"22,521 square feet"},{"term":"Retreat, athletics and children","detail":"A full-size sports court with locker rooms, a game room and a flexible hall.","note":"18,514 square feet"},{"term":"Residential","detail":"One-bedroom apartments over the two upper floors of Building B, with a common room on each.","note":"17,889 square feet"},{"term":"Diocese","detail":"Offices, a conference room and a lounge in Building C.","note":"7,240 square feet"},{"term":"Plant","detail":"Mechanical, electrical and the rest of what a building this size needs and nobody sees.","note":"1,503 square feet"}]}]},{"ground":"plain","blocks":[{"t":"eyebrow","text":"Five and a half acres","slot":"the-land.eyebrow"},{"t":"title","text":"How it sits on the land","slot":"the-land.h2"},{"t":"lede","text":"The lot is the one the church already owns — Map 40, Lot 008, between Rice Road and Turkey Hill Road. No land is being bought, and the buildings are fitted to what is there.","slot":"the-land.lede"},{"t":"prose","paragraphs":["The three buildings are set back from both streets and turned inward, so that what they enclose is a courtyard rather than a frontage. The car park wraps around the outside: 190 spaces, six of them accessible, which is exactly what the Wayland bylaw asks for a church of 570 seats, and a fire access road runs the length of the north boundary.","Built footprint comes to 18 per cent of the lot against a limit of 20, and just under two acres is left as open space. A brook crosses the north-west corner of the property, and the drawings show the work inside its buffer measured, drawn and kept small — a little over two thousand square feet of it. Two species of native cedar are specified for the planting.","The present church is drawn on the site plan as a dashed outline marked *outline of existing church to be demolished*. That is the hardest line on the sheet, and it is the honest one: the congregation would be worshipping in the building on the day the drawings for its replacement are approved."],"slot":"the-land.body"}]},{"ground":"sunken","blocks":[{"t":"eyebrow","text":"Nothing has been dug","slot":"where-it-stands.eyebrow"},{"t":"title","text":"Where the work stands","slot":"where-it-stands.h2"},{"t":"lede","text":"Dates below are the ones printed on the drawings themselves, not dates anybody remembers.","slot":"where-it-stands.lede"},{"t":"timeline","items":[{"when":"2016","what":"St. Mark of Boston buys the Church of the Holy Spirit at 169 Rice Road, and this church begins — in a building designed for somebody else’s liturgy."},{"when":"June 2025","what":"The architects issue the first renderings of the church itself — Building A, on its own, without the site around it."},{"when":"August 2025","what":"The full set goes to the Town of Wayland: three buildings, forty-odd sheets, and seven consulting firms named on the cover — civil, structural, code, mechanical, transport, landscape."},{"when":"November 2025","what":"First revision, marked “per town review”, together with the survey of abutters the process requires."},{"when":"December 2025","what":"Second revision, again per town review, and the compiled set that this page was written from."},{"when":"March 2026","what":"The flythrough above is delivered — the first time the three buildings are shown in colour, with materials on them."},{"when":"Next","what":"Site plan approval from the Wayland Planning Board. The endorsement line on the cover sheet is still blank, and every drawing in the set is stamped “not for construction”. Nothing has been dug."}]},{"t":"prose","paragraphs":["Every sheet in the set is stamped *not for construction*, and the line on the cover reading *date site plan endorsed* is still blank. The design has been revised twice already in answer to the town’s review, and it can be revised again. What is on this page is a proposal in front of a planning board, which is a real and specific stage and is not the same thing as a building.","Building it is the work of years and of the congregation’s [offering](/giving). If you want to know where it has got to since this page was written, [ask the office](/contact) — that answer will be current and this one will not."],"slot":"where-it-stands.foot"}]},{"ground":"tinted","blocks":[{"t":"eyebrow","text":"Nine firms","slot":"who-is-drawing-it.eyebrow"},{"t":"title","text":"Who is drawing it","slot":"who-is-drawing-it.h2"},{"t":"lede","text":"A structural engineer, a traffic consultant, a code consultant, a wetland scientist and a licensed land surveyor, each named on the sheets they are responsible for. This is not a sketch somebody made for the church.","slot":"who-is-drawing-it.lede"},{"t":"list","items":[{"term":"Architects and planners","termSlot":"who-is-drawing-it.firm-dsk-role","detail":"Dewing Schmid Kearns","detailSlot":"who-is-drawing-it.firm-dsk-name","note":"Concord, Massachusetts","noteSlot":"who-is-drawing-it.firm-dsk-town"},{"term":"Civil engineering","termSlot":"who-is-drawing-it.firm-oak-consulting-group-role","detail":"Oak Consulting Group","detailSlot":"who-is-drawing-it.firm-oak-consulting-group-name","note":"Newburyport, Massachusetts","noteSlot":"who-is-drawing-it.firm-oak-consulting-group-town"},{"term":"Structural engineering","termSlot":"who-is-drawing-it.firm-foley-buhl-roberts-role","detail":"Foley Buhl Roberts & Associates","detailSlot":"who-is-drawing-it.firm-foley-buhl-roberts-name","note":"Portland, Maine","noteSlot":"who-is-drawing-it.firm-foley-buhl-roberts-town"},{"term":"Mechanical, electrical, plumbing and fire protection","termSlot":"who-is-drawing-it.firm-buro-happold-role","detail":"Buro Happold","detailSlot":"who-is-drawing-it.firm-buro-happold-name","note":"Boston, Massachusetts","noteSlot":"who-is-drawing-it.firm-buro-happold-town"},{"term":"Landscape architecture","termSlot":"who-is-drawing-it.firm-crowley-cottrell-role","detail":"Crowley Cottrell","detailSlot":"who-is-drawing-it.firm-crowley-cottrell-name","note":"Boston, Massachusetts","noteSlot":"who-is-drawing-it.firm-crowley-cottrell-town"},{"term":"Traffic and transport","termSlot":"who-is-drawing-it.firm-mdm-role","detail":"MDM Transportation Consultants","detailSlot":"who-is-drawing-it.firm-mdm-name","note":"Marlborough, Massachusetts","noteSlot":"who-is-drawing-it.firm-mdm-town"},{"term":"Building and fire code","termSlot":"who-is-drawing-it.firm-code-red-role","detail":"Code Red Consultants","detailSlot":"who-is-drawing-it.firm-code-red-name","note":"Southborough, Massachusetts","noteSlot":"who-is-drawing-it.firm-code-red-town"},{"term":"Wetland delineation and ecology","termSlot":"who-is-drawing-it.firm-goddard-consulting-role","detail":"Goddard Consulting","detailSlot":"who-is-drawing-it.firm-goddard-consulting-name"},{"term":"Land survey and existing conditions","termSlot":"who-is-drawing-it.firm-dillis-roy-role","detail":"Dillis & Roy Civil Design Group","detailSlot":"who-is-drawing-it.firm-dillis-roy-name","note":"Concord, Massachusetts","noteSlot":"who-is-drawing-it.firm-dillis-roy-town"}]},{"t":"small","text":"All views on this page are renderings by Dewing Schmid Kearns Architects + Planners, drawn for the church — eight of them frames from the flythrough of 19 March 2026, and two of them sheets from the set filed with the Town of Wayland in December 2025. Nothing here is a photograph, and nothing here is final."}]},{"ground":"deep","blocks":[{"t":"title","text":"Paying for it","slot":"paying-for-it.h2"},{"t":"lede","text":"The drawings are paid for. The buildings are not, and they will be built the way everything in this church is built — out of what the congregation gives.","slot":"paying-for-it.lede"},{"t":"prose","paragraphs":["When the form opens, change the fund to **SPSM Construction Project**. It opens on the general fund, and an offering left there supports the church’s day-to-day work rather than this."],"slot":"paying-for-it.fund"},{"t":"give","text":"Give to the construction fund"},{"t":"small","text":"One offering or a recurring one, through the church’s Tithe.ly form. [More ways to give](/giving).","slot":"paying-for-it.note"}]}]},"welcome-newcomers":{"sections":[{"ground":"deep","blocks":[{"t":"eyebrow","text":"Follow the tour","slot":"tour-hero.eyebrow"},{"t":"title","text":"Six stations through the Coptic Orthodox Church","slot":"tour-hero.title"},{"t":"lede","text":"The same six stops the guides walk. Collect a stamp at each one.","slot":"tour-hero.lede"}]},{"ground":"plain","fold":true,"number":1,"blocks":[{"t":"title","text":"Origins of the Coptic Orthodox Church","slot":"station-1.h2"},{"t":"lede","text":"Where the word \"Coptic\" comes from, the calendar the Church still keeps, and the language it prayed in for a thousand years.","slot":"station-1.lede"},{"t":"subhead","text":"Two words: Coptic, and Orthodox"},{"t":"prose","paragraphs":["The Coptic Church was established in the name of the Lord Jesus Christ by St. Mark the Evangelist, in the city of Alexandria, around 43 A.D.","**Coptic** comes through Greek. The Greek word for Egypt was *Aigyptos*; when the Arabs reached Egypt in the seventh century they shortened it to *qibt*. Because the Egyptians they found there were Christians, the same word came to carry both meanings at once — Egyptian, and Christian.","**Orthodoxy** means keeping the original faith. The Creed settled at Nicaea in 325 A.D. has been attacked repeatedly in the centuries since, and the Coptic Church has defended it each time. At Nicaea itself the defence of Christ’s divinity was led by St. Athanasius, later the 20th Pope of Alexandria, who is called the Father of Orthodoxy and the Apostolic for it."]},{"t":"quote","label":"The Orthodox Creed, as printed in the Agpeya","paragraphs":["We believe in one God, God the Father, the Pantocrator, who created heaven and earth, and all things, seen and unseen.","We believe in one Lord Jesus Christ, the only-begotten Son of God, begotten of the Father before all ages. Light of Light, true God of true God, begotten, not created, of one essence with the Father, by whom all things were made; who, for us men and for our salvation, came down from heaven, and was incarnate of the Holy Spirit and of the Virgin Mary, and became man.","And He was crucified for us under Pontius Pilate, suffered and was buried. And on the third day He rose from the dead, according to the scriptures, ascended into the heavens and sat at the right hand of His Father; and He is coming again in His glory to judge the living and the dead, whose kingdom shall have no end.","Yes, we believe in the Holy Spirit, the Lord, the Giver of Life, who proceeds from the Father; who, with the Father and the Son, is worshipped and glorified; who spoke by the prophets.","And in one holy, catholic and apostolic Church. We confess one baptism for the remission of sins. We look for the resurrection of the dead, and the life of the age to come. Amen."]},{"t":"subhead","text":"The Coptic language"},{"t":"prose","paragraphs":["The first Christians in Egypt prayed in Coptic — not a borrowed language but their own, the ancient Egyptian of the pharaohs carried forward and written in Greek letters, with seven extra letters kept from Demotic for the sounds Greek had no use for. It remained the Church’s main language until the eleventh century, and it is still what the hymns are sung in.","Here is one of them."]},{"t":"elsewhere","text":"A Coptic hymn, to listen to, is on the church’s website."},{"t":"prose","paragraphs":["Write your own name in the same letters:"]},{"t":"elsewhere","text":"Writing your name in Coptic letters is on the church’s website."},{"t":"subhead","text":"The Coptic calendar"},{"t":"prose","paragraphs":["The Alexandrian calendar is the oldest calendar in history, and it was set by the stars. The Egyptians watched for the star they called Sothis — Sirius — to reappear at dawn, and fixed the year by it.","Its count of years begins in 284 A.D. and is called *Anno Martyrum*, the Year of the Martyrs. That date is the start of the reign of the Emperor Diocletian, who is remembered for persecuting Christians more savagely than any emperor before him. Choosing it was deliberate. The Church dates itself from the worst thing that was ever done to it, because what the years count from is the point at which dying stopped being a reason to stop believing.","The month names are the ancient Egyptian ones, each named for a god held to govern that stretch of the farming year, and the year divides into three seasons of the Nile with one short month left over:"]},{"t":"elsewhere","text":"The Coptic year, month by month, is on the church’s website."},{"t":"subhead","text":"Relics"},{"t":"prose","paragraphs":["A relic is a fragment associated with a saint, kept by the Church because the saint is a role model and not a memory. On the saint’s feast day the relics are brought out during the service and carried in procession while the melody belonging to that saint is sung."]}]},{"ground":"plain","fold":true,"number":2,"blocks":[{"t":"title","text":"Icons & Architecture","slot":"station-2.h2"},{"t":"lede","text":"Why the faces in an icon do not look like photographs, and what the wooden screen across the front of the church is for.","slot":"station-2.lede"},{"t":"subhead","text":"What an icon is"},{"t":"prose","paragraphs":["*Icon* is simply the Greek word for image. The first images of Christ were made during His lifetime, by people who had seen Him and followed Him; the Evangelist Luke is held to have been among the first to paint and to sculpt Him, and is credited by tradition with images of the bleeding woman who was healed by touching Him.","An icon holds past, present and future at once — the way God sees them — which is why nothing in one is set at a particular hour of a particular day."]},{"t":"subhead","text":"Three images tradition says were not painted"},{"t":"fold","label":"The Veil of St. Veronica","text":"On the road to the cross","paragraphs":["A cloth pressed to the face of Christ as He carried the cross, which kept the impression of it."]},{"t":"fold","label":"The Image of Edessa","text":"Sent to a king who was ill","paragraphs":["Christ sent a cloth marked with His own face to the King of Edessa, who was suffering from an illness."]},{"t":"fold","label":"The Shroud of Turin","text":"Fourteen feet of linen","paragraphs":["A linen cloth fourteen feet long bearing the image of a crucified man, front and back."]},{"t":"subhead","text":"Why the faces do not look like photographs"},{"t":"prose","paragraphs":["Orthodox icons are not attempts at likeness, and the flatness is not a want of skill. During the Italian Renaissance painters were praised for rendering the human body convincingly — its weight, its muscle, its light. An icon refuses that on purpose. The subject is the transfigured body, not the physical one, and a painter who draws the eye to the physique has painted the wrong thing.","Everyone perceives God differently, so an icon is drawn simply and leaves room. Less is more here: an icon is a diagram, not a portrait, and it is not claiming to show what Jesus or the saints looked like."]},{"t":"subhead","text":"The nave, and the barrier across it"},{"t":"prose","paragraphs":["The nave is where the congregation stands and sits to pray. The word is the word for a ship — everyone in it is on a journey to the Father, together, in the same vessel.","Between the nave and the sanctuary there is usually a wooden barrier, and the icons set into it are what bridge the gap: St. Mary as mother to Jesus, Jesus as the doorway to the Father, St. John the Baptist as the last of the prophets, saying *make a path in the wilderness and get ready for the Lord*. The barrier separates, and the icons on it are how you come to know the One on the other side."]},{"t":"subhead","text":"Reading one icon: St. Mark"},{"t":"prose","paragraphs":["St. Mark is this church’s founder, and his icon is the one worth learning to read. He was young and always close to the Lord — one of the seventy rather than the Twelve, but near enough that he was at the wedding at Cana, and near enough that he is the man carrying the jar of water whom two disciples were sent to follow when they went to prepare the Passover. The room they prepared was in his own house (Mark 14:13–14; Luke 22:11).","Four things in the picture, and what each is doing there:"]},{"t":"fold","label":"The loose sandal strap","text":"Alexandria, on arrival","paragraphs":["His sandal strap broke as he came into the city. The cobbler mending it, Anianos, drove the awl through his own hand and cried out *O One God* — St. Mark healed the hand, and Anianos was converted, and then his household, and then a great many more. The Church in Egypt starts with a broken strap."]},{"t":"fold","label":"The lighthouse and the sea","text":"Alexandria itself","paragraphs":["The Pharos, and the harbour it stood in. It is how the icon says *this happened in Alexandria*."]},{"t":"fold","label":"The angel with a crown","text":"In some icons","paragraphs":["His martyrdom. An angel appeared to him in prison to tell him his hour had come, and the crown is what a martyr receives."]},{"t":"fold","label":"The lion","text":"Of the four creatures, his","paragraphs":["Mark opens his Gospel with John the Baptist as a voice crying in the wilderness — a lion roaring in the desert (Mark 1:3).","There is also the story from Jordan, where a lion and a lioness came at Mark and his father, and Mark’s prayer struck the beasts dead; his father was converted by it. Of the four incorporeal creatures, Mark is the lion: roaring in the desert with prophetic power, proclaiming Jesus as king."]}]},{"ground":"plain","fold":true,"number":3,"blocks":[{"t":"title","text":"Ranks in the Church, and What They Wear","slot":"station-3.h2"},{"t":"lede","text":"Every garment worn at the altar means something. Here is what each one is and who wears it.","slot":"station-3.lede"},{"t":"subhead","text":"Who wears what"},{"t":"prose","paragraphs":["Nothing worn at the altar is decoration. Every garment is a statement about the person wearing it, and a priest and a deacon are dressed differently because they are doing different things."]},{"t":"list","label":"Priest","items":[{"term":"The Tunic — Tonia"},{"term":"The Presbyterial or Episcopal Stole — Sadra"},{"term":"The Sleeves — Epimanikia"},{"term":"The Girdle — Al Mintaqa"},{"term":"The Cope — Bornos"},{"term":"The Taylassan"}]},{"t":"list","label":"Deacon","items":[{"term":"The Tunic — Tonia"},{"term":"The Stole — Badrashil"}]},{"t":"subhead","text":"The garments, one at a time"},{"t":"fold","label":"The Tunic","text":"Tonia","paragraphs":["The white robe. White for the purity asked of anyone serving God — a colour the Bible returns to again and again for the same reason.","It is long, from the neck to the ankles, and the length is the meaning: God’s grace covering over the sins of the one who is serving."]},{"t":"fold","label":"The Stole","text":"Badrashil","paragraphs":["A strip of coloured, embroidered cloth, and a sign of service and obedience. The word is Greek, for a grace a person takes up and carries on the shoulders.","Deacons and archdeacons wear it over the left shoulder, brought down under the right elbow, with the ends hanging front and back — a wide red ribbon around the shoulders. It reverses to dark blue, purple or black for funerals and for Holy Week.","Not everyone in the choir wears one. The Chanter *(Epsaltos)* wears the tunic without it. The Reader *(Anagnostes)* and the Subdeacon *(Epi-diakon)* wear it crossed over the back and wrapped round to the front like a belt, both ends hanging from the shoulders: crossed because he shares in carrying the cross with Christ, belted as a sign of readiness for service — John the Baptist girding his waist."]},{"t":"fold","label":"The Stole of a priest","text":"Sadra","paragraphs":["The presbyterial or episcopal stole, which answers to the breastplate Aaron was commanded to wear (Exodus 28). It is essentially the deacon’s stole worn differently — around the neck instead of over one shoulder, both ends hanging in front. The two ends were later joined into a single strip down the front, and the seam where they were joined is still visible."]},{"t":"fold","label":"The Hat","text":"Em’ma","paragraphs":["Worn by bishops and priests, and originally a cap with a turban wound around it. It means one thing, and it means it at the person wearing it: that a bishop, a priest, a pope, however much authority he has been given, is still under God’s."]}]},{"ground":"plain","fold":true,"number":4,"blocks":[{"t":"title","text":"Cymbals, Triangle & the Midnight Praises","slot":"station-4.h2"},{"t":"lede","text":"The two instruments the Coptic Church plays, how they are held, and the all-night service they belong to.","slot":"station-4.lede"},{"t":"subhead","text":"Two instruments, and no others"},{"t":"prose","paragraphs":["The Coptic Church plays percussion and nothing else: small hand cymbals — *daff* in Arabic — and a metal triangle. A deacon plays them, with the *ma’lm*, the cantor who leads the singing.","They are not accompaniment in the ordinary sense. They keep the congregation together, mark where a hymn turns, and tell several hundred people when to come in — which is most of what an instrument is for in a church with no organ and no choir stalls.","Both are scriptural. Hand cymbals appear in the Old Testament and the New — Psalm 150, and 1 Corinthians 13:1 — and the triangle is in the Book of Samuel."]},{"t":"subhead","text":"What the cymbals are, and how they are played"},{"t":"prose","paragraphs":["A pair of slightly concave metal disks, five to seven inches across, with a cupped centre about an inch and a quarter deep. A hole through the middle takes a string, held in place by a pin, and the pin is the handle."]},{"t":"fold","label":"The slide","text":"Diagonal","paragraphs":["The disks are drawn diagonally across each other rather than struck together. It is the deeper of the two sounds."]},{"t":"fold","label":"The circle","text":"Rim on rim","paragraphs":["The rims are turned against one another in a circular motion. Alternating this with the slide is what gives a single pair of cymbals more than one voice."]},{"t":"fold","label":"The ending","text":"Trill, then clap","paragraphs":["A hymn is closed with a trill on the rims and a single clap. When you hear it, the hymn has finished — that is the signal the congregation is listening for."]},{"t":"aside","text":"At the festival, the youth musicians play these here. Ask them to show you the difference between the two strokes."},{"t":"subhead","text":"Tasbeha — the midnight praises"},{"t":"prose","paragraphs":["Tasbeha is the praise the Church keeps at night, and part of it is sung with the cymbals and the triangle. It is the service that runs from late evening towards morning, and it is where most Copts learn the hymns they know by heart."]}]},{"ground":"plain","fold":true,"number":5,"blocks":[{"t":"title","text":"The Ney, and the Oldest Music in Egypt","slot":"station-5.h2"},{"t":"lede","text":"A reed flute that has been played without a break for something like five thousand years.","slot":"station-5.lede"},{"t":"subhead","text":"The ney"},{"t":"prose","paragraphs":["The ney is an end-blown flute — a length of reed with six holes in front and one behind, and no mouthpiece at all. It is central to Persian, Turkish, Jewish and Arabic music, and in several of those traditions it is the only wind instrument used.","It has been played continuously for something between four and a half and five thousand years, back to ancient Egypt, which makes it one of the oldest instruments still in use anywhere in the world. The musicians painted on the walls of Egyptian tombs are playing an instrument a player today would recognise.","Because there is no mouthpiece, the sound is made entirely by the player’s lips against the rim of the reed. That is why it takes years to get a note out of one, and why it sounds like breath."]},{"t":"subhead","text":"And the oud"},{"t":"prose","paragraphs":["The ney’s usual companion: a short-necked lute with a bent-back pegbox and no frets, which is what gives it the sliding tones that a guitar cannot make."]}]},{"ground":"plain","fold":true,"number":6,"blocks":[{"t":"title","text":"The Liturgy, the Sacraments & What Is on the Altar","slot":"station-6.h2"},{"t":"lede","text":"What each object on the altar is for, the seven sacraments, and why the whole service is told as a wedding.","slot":"station-6.lede"},{"t":"subhead","text":"What is on the altar"},{"t":"fold","label":"The Censer","text":"And the incense in it","paragraphs":["Three chains, for the Holy Trinity. The censer with its coal is St. Mary’s womb, carrying Jesus; the priest raising it is prayer going up to heaven; and the bells on it are there to keep the people praying awake to what they are doing.","Incense runs through both Testaments — the angel appearing to Zechariah in the temple as he offered it; frankincense among the first gifts brought to the infant Christ; and in Revelation, John seeing an angel burning incense in a golden censer (Rev 8:3–4)."],"items":[{"term":"The upper dome","detail":"heaven"},{"term":"The lower dome","detail":"the womb of St. Mary"},{"term":"The coal","detail":"Christ’s humanity"},{"term":"The fire","detail":"Christ’s divinity"}]},{"t":"fold","label":"The Throne","text":"The Ark","paragraphs":["A box at the centre of the altar — in Coptic *pi totc*, a seat or a throne — cubicle-shaped with high flaps, which the chalice stands in. It is called the Ark because the life-giving Blood it holds answers to the pot of manna kept in the Ark of the Covenant, which was itself a sign of the true Body of Christ."]},{"t":"fold","label":"The Chalice","text":"The Communion Cup","paragraphs":["Wine mixed with water, consecrated into the Blood of the Lord during the Liturgy of the Eucharist — following Christ’s own use of the cup (Matthew 26:26–27) and the Apostles’ after Him. St. Paul calls it the Cup of Blessing and the Cup of the Lord (1 Corinthians 10:16, 21)."]},{"t":"fold","label":"The Paten","text":"A plain round tray","paragraphs":["Silver or gold, without a stand and without engraving. It stands for the Lord’s manger and for His tomb — the two places He was laid. Christ used it at the Last Supper (Matthew 26:26–27), and the Apostles after Him (1 Corinthians 10:16)."]},{"t":"fold","label":"The Orban","text":"The communion bread","paragraphs":["Round, because a circle has no beginning and no end, as Christ is everlasting. A large cross in the centre is Jesus Christ; the twelve smaller crosses around it are the twelve disciples.","Five holes are pierced through it, for His wounds: three for the nails in the hands and the feet, one for the crown of thorns, one for the spear in His side.","Around the edge it is printed in Coptic — *Agios Otheos, Agios Esheros, Agios Athanatos*: Holy God, Holy Mighty, Holy Immortal."]},{"t":"subhead","text":"The seven sacraments"},{"t":"prose","paragraphs":["A sacrament is an invisible grace received through a visible rite — a sign or a substance, administered by a priest. There are seven, and each has its own."]},{"t":"fold","label":"Baptism","text":"Water","paragraphs":["Instituted by Christ’s own baptism in the Jordan, by what He said to Nicodemus (John 3), and by what He commanded the Apostles (Matthew 28:19)."]},{"t":"fold","label":"Confirmation","text":"The oil of Myron","paragraphs":["Rooted in John 7:37–39 and Acts 8:14–17. As the believers grew too many for the Apostles to lay hands on each one, the Myron was developed — oil mixed with the spices that had been used to embalm the body of Christ — so that priests could anoint new believers to the same effect."]},{"t":"fold","label":"Confession","text":"The prayer of absolution","paragraphs":["Repentance. The sign is the confessing person receiving the prayer of absolution. Matthew 18:18; John 20:21–22."]},{"t":"fold","label":"Eucharist","text":"Bread and wine","paragraphs":["Transformed into the Body and Blood of Christ. Instituted at the Last Supper (Mark 14:22–24)."]},{"t":"fold","label":"Unction of the Sick","text":"Oil","paragraphs":["For healing, of the body and of the mind. Matthew 10:8; Luke 10:8; Mark 6:13; James 5:14–15."]},{"t":"fold","label":"Holy Matrimony","text":"The prayers over the couple","paragraphs":["The sign is the bride and groom receiving the prayers that unite them in body and in spirit. Instituted by Christ’s presence at the wedding at Cana (John 2:1–11); St. Paul calls marriage a great mystery, reflecting Christ and the Church (Ephesians 5:32)."]},{"t":"fold","label":"Priesthood","text":"The laying on of hands","paragraphs":["The sign is the ordained person receiving the prayers and the laying on of hands. Instituted when Christ chose the Twelve after a night of prayer (Luke 6:12–13) and sent them out (Matthew 28:19–20; John 20:23)."]},{"t":"subhead","text":"The liturgy, told as a wedding"},{"t":"prose","paragraphs":["This is how the shape of the service is taught: not as a list of parts but as a courtship, in order, each stage following from the one before it."]},{"t":"steps","items":[{"term":"The Groom’s invitation","note":"Vespers, Matins and the midnight praises","paragraphs":["The Groom knocks, to say that the proposal is coming. The bride — the Church — cannot sleep, and stays up praising Him all night.","*Arise, O you children of the night, let us praise the Lord of Hosts, that He may grant us the salvation of our souls.*"]},{"term":"The meeting","note":"The Offering of the Lamb","paragraphs":["The Groom arrives, and formally proposes."]},{"term":"The dialogue","note":"The readings","paragraphs":["The bride’s family asks to know Him better. The Church listens — the Pauline, the Catholic, the Praxis, the Synaxarium, the Psalm and the Gospel — and in them He tells them His life and what He believes."]},{"term":"Reconciliation","note":"Before the union proceeds","paragraphs":["Whatever stands between the Groom and the bride is taken out of the way first. Nothing goes further until it is."]},{"term":"The marriage","note":"The Eucharistic Liturgy","paragraphs":["The bride asks whether He really loves her. He answers by being broken into pieces for her, in the breaking of the bread — and she takes His Body and His Blood. That is the union, and it is where the service has been going the whole time."]}]},{"t":"aside","text":"Which is why the liturgy is not called a ritual but a battle of the morning: something to be prepared for and taken part in, not attended. Servants and parents are asked to help children taste and experience Christ here — not simply to bring them."}]}]}}
